<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Posidonius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 135–c. 51 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Posidonius of Rhodes, Posidonius of Apamea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Posidonius was a Greek Stoic philosopher, historian and scientist from Apamea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> who settled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, where he ran a famous school and held public office. He wrote on philosophy, history, geography, astronomy and the tides, and estimated the circumference of the Earth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> visited him, and his works, now lost, influenced many later writers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/posidonius.jpg" alt="Posidonius" width="320" height="586" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Posidonio,_replica_augustea_(23_ac.-14_dc_ca)_da_originale_del_100-50_ac._ca._6142.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Posidonius, nicknamed the Athlete, was born around 135 BCE into a Greek family in Apamea, a Hellenistic city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontes-river">Orontes River</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. As a young man he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and studied under Panaetius, the leading Stoic philosopher of the age. After Panaetius died in 110 BCE he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, gained citizenship, and kept a school there that became the leading institution of its time.</p><p>From about the 90s BCE he made voyages round the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> to gather scientific data and observe peoples. At Gades on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> he saw tides much higher than in the Mediterranean and held that daily tides follow the Moon's orbit. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> he studied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> and noted that they honoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druid">Druids</a>, whom he saw as philosophers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On Rhodes Posidonius held office as one of the Prytaneis, the most important political office there. He was chosen for at least one embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in 87–86 BCE, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mithridatic-war">First Mithridatic War</a>. Under him Rhodes eclipsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> as the centre of Stoic philosophy, drawing Greek and Roman students.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> attended a lecture in 66 BCE and again in 62 BCE, when Posidonius, suffering from gout, answered his pain by declaring that it would never persuade him it was an evil. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> attended his lectures in his twenties and called him 'my teacher'; Posidonius politely declined to write on Cicero's consulship. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Nysa</a> succeeded him as head of the school.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His works survive only in fragments, though the titles of more than twenty are known. He wrote on physics, astronomy, geography, history, ethics and mathematics, and held that philosophy was the master art that alone could explain the cosmos. A Stoic who also drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, he saw the universe as an interconnected whole. He estimated the Sun's distance at 9,893 times the Earth's radius and reasoned that the Moon causes the tides.</p><p>In his Histories he continued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>'s account of Roman expansion, covering 146–88 BCE in 52 volumes. His lost geography of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> was a main source for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, Caesar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>. Strabo called him the most learned of the philosophers of his time.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Posidonius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The orator Aeschines is said to have taught there in exile, the Stoic philosophers Panaetius and <b>Posidonius</b> were closely associated with the island, and Apollonius Molon taught rhetoric to Cicero.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Pliny the Elder, Lucan, Strabo, <b>Posidonius</b> and Diodorus Siculus added rites and sacrifices, but such writers saw the Celts as “barbarians”, and the Romans had reason to make them seem uncivilised.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Posidonius</b>, a Greek who travelled in Gaul about 100 BCE and whose account survives through Diodorus Siculus, Strabo and Athenaeus, wrote of feasts at which the bravest man claimed the best cut of meat and rivals fought him for it, and Diodorus says that Italian merchants could get…</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Homer's Odyssey placed the Cimmerians in a sunless land at the entrance of Hades, a poetic image that later writers moved to Italy, the far north or Hyperborea, and <b>Posidonius</b> equated them with the Cimbri.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cimmerians">Cimmerians · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apamea-syria">Apamea</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.42,36.4&amp;year=-81" aria-label="Apamea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5471 884 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Posidonius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5921 1154h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5921 1154h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.42,36.4&amp;year=-81">What was here in 81 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
